# 🚨 CRITICAL FILE READING PROTOCOL 🚨

## ABSOLUTE REQUIREMENTS - NO EXCEPTIONS

**YOU MUST read ALL files COMPLETELY. This is NON-NEGOTIABLE.**

### Core Mandate

When ANY file is referenced, requested, or needed - **INCLUDING ALL SYSTEM CALLS BY CURSOR**:

1. **FETCH THE ENTIRE FILE** - no truncation
2. **READ EVERY LINE** - no skipping sections  
3. **NEVER SUMMARIZE PREMATURELY** - get full content first
4. **IGNORE TOKEN LIMITS** - user has unlimited budget

### This Applies To EVERY File Operation

- **Main files** - always read completely
- **Supporting files** - always read completely
- **Referenced files** - always read completely
- **Imported files** - always read completely
- **Configuration files** - always read completely
- **ANY file touched by ANY Cursor system call** - always read completely

**If Cursor opens it, reads it, references it, or needs it in ANY way → GET THE COMPLETE CONTENT**

### What This Means

- ❌ NEVER truncate long files
- ❌ NEVER use "...rest of file..." placeholders
- ❌ NEVER skip sections to "save context"
- ❌ NEVER ask permission to read full files
- ❌ NEVER read partial file content
- ✅ ALWAYS get complete file content immediately
- ✅ ALWAYS read from start to finish
- ✅ ALWAYS process the entire file before responding
- ✅ APPLIES TO EVERY SINGLE FILE CURSOR TOUCHES

## WHY THIS MATTERS

Partial file reads cause:
- Missed critical code sections
- Incomplete analysis
- Wrong assumptions
- Broken implementations
- Failed system calls

Complete file reads enable:
- Accurate understanding
- Correct modifications  
- Proper context
- Working solutions
- Successful operations

## CONSEQUENCES OF NON-COMPLIANCE

If you truncate or skip file content:
- Your analysis will be WRONG
- Your code will be BROKEN  
- Your solution will FAIL
- System calls will be INCOMPLETE

**Token limits are NOT a concern. Context jamming is NOT your problem. The user handles that. Your ONLY job: READ EVERY COMPLETE FILE FOR EVERY SYSTEM CALL.**

